WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Maya Chen: Good morning, Robert. Thanks for making the time. I wanted to talk through the proceeds from the sale and where you'd like to go from here.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Robert Alvarez: Morning, Maya. Yes, it's been on my mind. The sale closed three weeks ago and the money is just sitting in checking. About 3.2 million after escrow released.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Maya Chen: Congratulations again, that's a big milestone. Before we talk about any products, what do you want this money to do for you?

4
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Robert Alvarez: Honestly, I want to stop working in about eight years. I'm 54. My daughter starts college in four years, so that has to be covered. And my wife wants a place at the lake in the next two years, somewhere around 600 thousand.

5
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Maya Chen: That's helpful. So three goals on three timelines: the lake house in about two years, college in four, retirement in eight.

6
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Robert Alvarez: Right. And I don't want to mess this up. In 2022 I had a lot in tech stocks and lost almost 35 percent. I didn't sleep for weeks. I'm not doing that again.

7
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Maya Chen: I hear you. If the portfolio dropped 15 percent in a bad year, how would you feel?

8
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Robert Alvarez: Uncomfortable, but I'd live with it as long as I understood why. Twenty percent, I'd probably want out.

9
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Maya Chen: That's a really useful answer. Do you need the portfolio to pay you anything today?

10
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Robert Alvarez: Not today. The buyer is paying me as a consultant for two more years. But I like the idea of something that pays steady income, like dividends.

11
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Maya Chen: Okay. And taxes. You'll have a sizable capital gains bill from the sale in April. Have you set that aside?

12
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Robert Alvarez: My CPA said around 700 thousand. I haven't moved it anywhere. Should I?

13
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Maya Chen: I'd keep that in something safe and liquid, like Treasury bills, so it earns something until April. And given your bracket, municipal bonds could make sense for part of the longer-term money, since the income is federally tax-free.

14
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Robert Alvarez: My brother-in-law keeps talking about munis. I like that. What about stocks? Everyone says the market is expensive right now.

15
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Maya Chen: For retirement money with an eight-year horizon, a diversified mix of stocks and bonds is still the core. We'd keep it broad, not concentrated in one sector like last time. Quality dividend payers would fit what you said about steady income.

16
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Robert Alvarez: That sounds sensible. And fees? I've heard horror stories.

17
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Maya Chen: Fair question. Every fee will be in writing in the proposal, so there are no surprises.

18
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Robert Alvarez: Good. What about the college money?

19
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Maya Chen: A 529 plan is the natural place. It grows tax-free for education, and with four years to go we'd keep it fairly conservative.

20
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Robert Alvarez: My wife asked about that too. Let's do it.

21
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Maya Chen: There's also a private credit fund we offer to qualified clients. Higher yield, but the money is locked up for five years.

22
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Robert Alvarez: Five years? I don't know. I want the lake house money available. Maybe later, once I see how the rest goes.

23
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Maya Chen: Completely reasonable. We'll leave it out of the first proposal.

24
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Robert Alvarez: I appreciate that you're not pushing. I've banked here fifteen years. I'd rather keep it all here if the plan makes sense.

25
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Maya Chen: Thank you, that means a lot. Here's what I suggest. I'll have a proposal to you by Friday: the tax reserve in T-bills, the lake house money in a short-term bond ladder, a 529 for your daughter, and a diversified retirement portfolio with a municipal bond sleeve. Could you send me last year's tax return so we can size the muni allocation?

26
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Robert Alvarez: I'll send it tonight. Can we bring my CPA into the next call? I want the tax side looked at properly.

27
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.000
Maya Chen: Absolutely. I'll set up a call with your CPA next week, and I'll send you a short risk questionnaire to confirm what you told me today.

28
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Robert Alvarez: Perfect. Thanks, Maya. I feel a lot better than I did this morning.

29
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Maya Chen: Glad to hear it. Talk Friday.
